{# One group of help articles: an icon, a heading, and the rows under it.

   Shared by the two places that draw a group -- the hub, which draws all of
   them, and the foot of an article, which draws the one it belongs to. They
   were the same markup twice over as soon as the article's card gained an
   icon, and the alignment below depends on the icon being exactly where the
   other card puts it, which is not a thing to keep true by hand in two files.

   The card around this differs and stays with the caller: a section on the
   hub, the article's own aside at the foot of a page. What is passed in:

     group       - slug, title, summary, icon, accent, articles
     heading_id  - what the card's aria-labelledby points at
     summary     - whether to print the group's sentence. The hub does; an
                   article does not, because by the time a reader reaches the
                   foot of one they have read the thing the sentence describes.
#}
<div class="help-group__head">
    <span class="help-group__icon help-group__icon--{{ group.accent }}">
        <i class="fas {{ group.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    </span>
    <div class="help-group__heading">
        <h2 class="help-group__title" id="{{ heading_id }}">{{ group.title }}</h2>
        {% if summary|default(false) %}
            <p class="help-group__summary">{{ group.summary }}</p>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>

{# Titles and no summaries. The old hub gave each of five articles a summary
   because there was nothing else to tell them apart; the group and its
   sentence do that job now, and nine summaries stacked in one column is a page
   nobody reads to the bottom of. #}
<ul class="help-group__list">
    {% for article in group.articles %}
        <li class="help-group__item">
            <a class="help-group__link" href="{{ article.url }}">
                <span class="help-group__label">{{ article.title }}</span>
                <i class="fas fa-chevron-right help-group__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </a>
        </li>
    {% endfor %}
</ul>
